It's possible that the `total` value is not being updated correctly because of race conditions that can occur if multiple edits happen too quickly. For example, if two users try to edit the same document at the same time, both of their updates may not be applied correctly due to the way Firestore handles concurrency.

To prevent this issue, you can use a Firestore transaction to ensure that the document is updated atomically without overwriting any changes made by other clients.

Here's an example of how you could modify your `initUserExpenses` function to use a transaction:

```kotlin
fun initUserExpenses(
    name: String,
    quantity: Int,
    expense: Double,
    collectionName: String,
    documentId: String
) {
    val db = FirebaseFirestore.getInstance()
    val docRef = db.collection("expenses")
        .document("Kantin")
        .collection(collectionName)
        .document(documentId)

    val initData = mapOf(
        "name" to name,
        "quantity" to quantity,
        "expenses" to expense
    )

    db.runTransaction { transaction ->
        val document = transaction.get(docRef)

        if (!document.exists()) {
            // Create the document if it doesn't exist
            transaction.set(docRef, initData)
        } else {
            // Update the existing document
